courses.learning_outcomes:
Upon completion of this course, students are expected to:

1. Understand management control environment and process, and their applications such as transfer pricing and incentive systems;

2. Understand the use of relevant accounting information in of management control system design and decision making.

After completing this course, students should be able to:

(1) demonstrate an understanding of the different elements of corporate governance under the Hong Kong and international setting and appreciate the significance of governance in the effective management and control of (i) organisations and (ii) the resources for which they are accountable;
(2) evaluate the role of governance and risk management in internal control, review and compliance;
(3) explain the role of accountant in (i) identifying, assessing, controlling and mitigating risks through implementation of internal controls and (ii) delivering appropriate recommendations;
(4) understand the nature of internal audit and its role in corporate governance, risk management, internal control and as part of overall performance management within an organisation;
(5) demonstrate an awareness of global contemporary compliance issues, in particular, fraud-related matters and environmental and social auditing; and

(6) demonstrate effective communication and interpersonal skills.

courses.course_syllabus:
Internal auditing can be described as a management-oriented discipline which has evolved rapidly. Such evolution is partially attributable to the emergence of corporate governance after a series of corporate scandals. A robust corporate governance regime within an organisation assists how the organisation operates and how business and strategic objectives can be attained. To achieve such, management needs to properly identify and manage a number of risks which may adversely affect the organisation’s success. This is the reason why risk management forms a cornerstone for corporate governance. The next question will be how risk management strategies can be executed. This then brings the significance of internal control activities to light. There is therefore an inter-locking relationship between corporate governance, risk management and internal control activities. This course attempts to investigate these three areas and highlight how internal auditing can bring values to an organisation in the abovementioned aspects.

The course is divided into three main blocks: (i) corporate and board governance under the requirements of the Hong Kong Listing Rules (and relevant overseas requirements); (ii) risk identification, assessment and management techniques from the perspective of internal auditors; and (iii) compliance risk management with a discussion of various current business issues such as forensic investigations, anti-bribery programmes and anti-money laundering techniques.

The objectives of the course are to:

(1) allow students to appreciate the relationships among corporate governance, risk management and internal controls;
(2) equip students with an understanding of the role of internal auditor in each of the above aspects and how an internal auditor can assist top management in achieving an organisation’s strategic goals;
(3) strengthen students’ awareness of the significance of risk management and internal controls in various important areas of operations;
(4) introduce concepts of various contemporary compliance and auditing-related issues; and

(5) strengthen students’ communication and interpersonal skills.

courses.learning_outcomes:
The learning objectives of the course are to:

(1) allow students to practically understand how computer-assisted audit techniques and data analytics help in the audit of financial statements;
(2) equip students with an understanding of the role of forensic accountants (or fraud examiners) and how a forensic accountant can assist top management in preventing, detecting and correcting frauds;
(3) strengthen students’ awareness of the significance of cyber risks in businesses and how accountants can help responding to such risks from a technical and practical perspective;
(4) strengthen students’ communication and interpersonal skills.

After completing this course, students will be able to:

1. acquire the knowledge of how capital markets function;
2. understand the roles of the regulator, the intermediaries and the investor;
3. appreciate the application and enforcement of laws and regulation;
4. be sensitised to compliance matters; and
5. recognise the challenges and opportunities as capital markets evolve.

After taking the course, students are expected to:

* (CLO1) understand the nature of ESG issues from different perspectives;
* (CLO2) explain the global trends of ESG and sustainability in accounting;
* (CLO3) describe the international and local standards of ESG reporting with reference to various reporting frameworks;
* (CLO4) analyse firms’ incentives of conducting and disclosing ESG activities;
* (CLO5) examine how ESG issues are linked to traditional accounting areas; and
* (CLO6) analyse material ESG and sustainability issues among companies.

After completing this course, students should be able to:
1. apply generic, contextual and specific research skills in identifying, structuring and dealing with modern financial accounting issues;
2. have an understanding of the legal, social, political and economic constraints that shape an accounting decision;
3. have an understanding of recent developments in accounting, particularly relating to the measurement perspective, fair value accounting, off-balance sheet liabilities earnings management, executive compensation and economic consequences of accounting;
4. have an enhanced mastery of quantitative and research techniques in studying accounting phenomena.
